ians prince and tom petty just to  name a few  no one is really prepared to use  fentanyl there's no such thing as a  long-term user  of fentanyl it's not just fentanyl the  production and use of all kinds of  synthetic drugs has surged in the last  five years as drugs manufactured in  illegal labs especially in mexico begin  to overtake plant-based products with  enough chemicals you can make those  drugs in staggering supplies all year  round no seasons no land you don't need  to worry about the sunlight or anything  like that kenona spent 10 years working  as a reporter in mexico then work for  the los angeles times before turning to  books  his 2015 bestseller dreamland was the  definitive account of how pharmaceutical  companies pushed painkillers like  oxycontin  heroin dealers followed in their wake  now canonist is out with the least of us  who are the least of us there are people  that are in need that we all can be like  them and given the right circumstances  or the wrong circumstances or the wrong  circumstances sure the book focuses on  potential solutions including programs  like the life learning center in  covington kentucky a building that once  helped build prisons is now helping keep  people out the bars of alcatraz were  made here yes alicia webb eddington is  the president of life learning you don't  like the term second chances for a  second chance why  transformational change transformational  employment all of us we've had more than  two chances in life  every one of us two chances we're gonna  limit people to two chances no we're  gonna transform people you also learn  some physical techniques for dealing  with negative thoughts this is not a  rehab facility it's a place where  addicts come to learn the skills to get  jobs there's even a clinic for free  tattoo removal  we met clayton carmack who spent years  abusing drugs and behind bars  why is it so important for you to get  these taken off  i can't erase all the things i've done i  can't erase the felonies they'll carry  with me for the rest of my life i'll  always have those things but being able  to walk into a room with you guys  one day and not have these tattoos that  are going to try to tell my story for me  i'm really grateful  this is an opportunity for us as a  community to make a difference webb  eddington is a former state trooper and  sheriff's deputy who for years worked  undercover narcotics she no longer  believes lock them up and throw away the  key is the answer i get some  chastisement from my fellow law  enforcement officers but also get a  great deal of support  you know alicia oh my god have you gone  soft on crime i haven't gone soft on  anything i've gone smart on it  one of the people who came here for help  is daniel woods a former golf pro who  got addicted to pills after three knee  surgeries and spent a total of 21 years  in prison it's called dodd that's up  containing dangerous drugs and one  doctor could not prescribe enough for me  it became a part to where i just seeked  out other doctors paper trail led up to  me and  i know it sounds corny but it saved my  life woods is now a life learning  employee  counseling others  if you didn't have this place where  would you be  well i can tell you i've been i've been  under bridges and been homeless  been scared to death where i would be at  today would uh  i don't even think about it  in the main hallway of life learning  there are pictures of the people who've  walked through their doors  667 this year alone if it was a drug  that could make you feel different  you were doing it  kenona's visits places like life  learning and the kenton county detention  center for insight on how to reframe our  understanding of addiction  and ways to treat it my whole life i've  been totally ignorant  about the facts of what addiction really  does to a human being do you have any  reason for hope right now  i i think so yeah i mean it's a pretty  dour panorama but  i do believe  that that we are being shown a lesson as  a country you're not done with this  subject  um i always think i am  but you know the thing is what i  realized during dreamland is that what  you're really you're i thought i was  writing about drugs and drug trafficking  and drug addiction no i really believe  it's  you have an opportunity through this  topic to write about america  we talked to sam and some of these  solutions are also pretty low tech it's  just getting outside and especially  during the pandemic so many people are  isolated it's spending time in the  community with friends family members  knowing that you're not alone first off  really great story important subject  matter i just know there's so many  people out there who wish that this  approach had been available to the  millions of people who were addicted to  cocaine and heroin and crack and got  excess and got incarcerated in the 90s  you know this is this is an approach  that looks like  it can work yeah and and the attitudes  are changing as we mentioned the piece  it used to be just like put them in  prison right forget about it clearly did  not work this is how you make change  jeff as michelle said great story

